A||||22||MooCow||3.4 pushrod  or   3.4 DOHC?||||1||
Z||000000||MooCow||08-28-2000||11:10 AM||||Okay well I have now decided to go with a 3.4 engine.  I don't really now the difference between the 3.4 pushrod or the DOHC.  Which is better and faster??  Do they each go in the same or different?? Also I plan on supercharging it and keeping my 4 speed stick and putting in a center force clutch.  So which engine should I use??<P>                            Thanks Ken||||reg||1
Z||000001||Philphine||08-28-2000||12:40 PM||||just on hearsay ( no real swap experience) i'd say the 4cam is the one to pick. seems to have more performance potential and better stock output.||||reg||1
Z||000002||theogre||08-28-2000||01:04 PM||||You should check with the super charger people you are going to use and see what they make parts for.<P>DOHC and how many valves per cyl. (Phil, I think he means 2 cams per motor, not 2 per head.) Over head cams on pick up some performance but they add a hell of allot of complication to the motor. It also depends on the cam followers and valve spring setup just how much more RPM the thing will take before the valves float. <P><BR>As for going in the same, most of that is up to the block but the whole intake likely will be differant on the DOHC. PTO device placement (Locations of Alternator, AC, etc.) may also be differant.||||reg||1
Z||000003||ThatRickGuy||08-28-2000||01:42 PM||||I've heard some people who really like the 3.4 DOHC, which I beleive is 4 cams also known as TDC (not Top Dead Center), 'Twin Dual Cam'. The only problems I've heard of with it is you have to cut one of the hood hinges, and they have been known to eat threw timing belts in 60k miles or less. The 3.4 TDC will be a much higher RPM engine, with a sacrifice of torque and low end, it'll get you plenty of top end HP. The 3.4 PR on the other hand will (with a few mods) bolt right up to the trany. It can also be set up in a variety of ways so that you can have tons of Torque   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> or spin it a little faster for the HP. Maybe I'm just biased, Every engine I've ever worked on has been an old push rod style, and I like them. <p>[This message has been edited by ThatRickGuy (edited 08-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Alex4mula||08-28-2000||02:39 PM||||3.4 pushrod is just a drop in and almost everything from the 2.8 bolts up except starter which requires drilling holes & some other minor mods depending on tranny.<P>3.4 DOHC requires a lot of modifications plus its computer, wiring, etc. For the hasle I would better do to a 3.8 swap instead of a 3.4 DOHC.<BR>If you want something straightfoward (&&lt;$$) then do the 3.4 pushrod.||||reg||1
Z||000005||Standard||08-28-2000||05:09 PM||||3.4 DOHC = 4 cams, 2 per head.  Just like any 4 cyl DOHC, it has seperate cams for intake and exhaust, but also 2 heads.  <P>DOHC revs higher and puts out more power.  It is a more difficult swap, tho. <P>------------------<BR>Mike -<BR>Black 86.5 GT 5 Spd, 149,000 miles and counting!      <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/BURNOUT.gif">   <BR>Red 84 Sport Coupe - winter donut maker||||reg||1
Z||000006||MooCow||08-28-2000||11:13 PM||||okay the pushrod sounds more appealing to me casue of the less work and the low end torque.  Now how much torque am I going to get wit ha super charger hook up??  Can I even supercharge it at all or not??  I want a 3.4 casue it is easy to do but still want to be able to smoke tires when wanted.<P>                                 Thanks Ken||||reg||10
Z||000007||mshill||08-29-2000||12:30 AM||||I also like the sound of the 3.4 pushrod, but were the 92-94 Camaros the only cars the engines were in?  If so, wouldn't they be difficult to find?  Where's the best place to get a used one?  We have lots of Pick-n-Pull yards in the Sacramento area, but I have yet to see a 3.4 in any of the Camaros or Firebirds in the yards.  Any other cars/years?||||reg||1
Z||000008||terryk||08-29-2000||01:07 AM||||There were tons of the 3.4L Camaro's built.<BR>Tons...... You won't find them in PNP's yet. At least not the one in Rocklin! Try Copartfinder.com.<P>Anyone know the VIN designation for the 3.4L engine?||||reg||4
Z||000009||Gokart||08-29-2000||06:48 AM||||Which 3.4 does Ed Park's install at his Fiero Factory?<P>Marc 87GT||||reg||5
Z||000010||3400Fiero||08-29-2000||10:18 AM||||I installed a 3400 DOHC in my 1988 formula by myself with no kit.  Was rather easy and I love this engine!  I would pick this motor again over a pushrod engine because it has higher low end torque and higher horsepower (220 lb-ft torque and 215 hp).  The 3.4 pushrod is 180 hp max.  It is a 4 cam 24 valve engine and as such has vastly more power potential than any pushrod 12 valve v6.  The only glitch with the installation is working out the wiring, but I am starting a web site to document everything and am happy to help anyone who wants to do this conversion - I have nothing to sell you.  I was rather irritated by the conversion companies who would not give any info unless I bought something from them.  <P>Just my two cents worth.<P>------------------<BR>1988 Fiero 3400DOHC<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||ThatRickGuy||08-29-2000||10:49 AM||||3.4 PR has a max hp of 180?!? I can get 180 out of my 3.1 just by swaping cams. as for low end torque, I don't have desktop dyno, but I'm willing to bet with a good cam, the PR will beet the TDC in torque, but the TDC will win in HP. the TDC was designed to breath faster and redline higher, compaired to a PR where running it over 5500 rpms will just make it starve. I am curious if you're running a manual trany? I would probrably say go with the TDC w/ a manual so you can take advantage of the higher rpm power band, or go 3.4 w/ the auto for more of a daily driver. <p>[This message has been edited by ThatRickGuy (edited 08-29-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Wannabe||08-29-2000||11:55 AM||||3400Fiero, you say the install was easy? I'd expect the swap to be mechanically straight forward, but have heard that the wide heads make it very difficult to work around the engine. How do you find it to be? Also, if you don't mind my asking, what did the TDC engine cost you in $CDN? The TDC is definitely the way to go for me, since the flexibility provided by the high rev range will be very useful for the time trials I run in. Also, the series rules allow engine swaps as long as the bore centers are the same as the original engine. Thank you GM!||||reg||1
Z||000013||LarryB||08-29-2000||11:57 AM||||Gokart, Ed Parks 3.4 installs are pushrods. There was an '88 Formula (?) with one Saturday @ Kickhill, I didn't take that long a look but from what I saw it was a nice clean install. ||||reg||1
Z||000014||Phil||08-29-2000||01:51 PM||||As far as I remember, at Kick Hill there was a 3400 DOHC(GT) and a SC3800(Formula)||||reg||1
Z||000015||Gokart||08-29-2000||09:35 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by LarryB:<BR><B>Gokart, Ed Parks 3.4 installs are pushrods. There was an '88 Formula (?) with one Saturday @ Kickhill, I didn't take that long a look but from what I saw it was a nice clean install. </B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I remember it now. It was clean. Looked stock. He had FOCOA jet coated headers on it too.<P><p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 08-29-2000).]||||reg||14
Z||000016||LarryB||08-29-2000||11:14 PM||||<BR>Right. That's the one.  <P>Phil, the car I'm talking about arrived I think in the afternoon and it was in the row across from your car all the way at the other end by the house, right near that blue '87. I know the 3.4 DOCH you're thinking of.  It was near the 4WD Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000017||3400Fiero||08-30-2000||10:11 AM||||[QUOTE]Originally posted by Wannabe:<BR>[B]3400Fiero, you say the install was easy? I'd expect the swap to be mechanically straight forward, but have heard that the wide heads make it very difficult to work around the engine. How do you find it to be? Also, if you don't mind my asking, what did the TDC engine cost you in $CDN? <P>I found the install to be mechanically easy, and the heads are a bit difficult to work around, but you get used to it.  The part that took some work was working out the wiring harness - all it takes are the stock wiring diagrams from both vehicles and time.  I got a really good deal on the engine.  I paid $500 CDN ($330 US) for the engine (not running, wiring harness and ECM (i'm in winnipeg)).  It took some work to get the engine running, but it is running fine now.  The entire conversion cost about $5000 CDN ($3300 US) mainly because I totally rebuilt the engine and am running a custom dual exhaust. I don't see how a conversion kit is worth $1800 US unless you have more money than time!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  I am now running 14.1 @ 104.4 MPH (measured by G-Tech)!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>------------------<BR>1988 Fiero 3400DOHC<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000018||fierocrazy||08-30-2000||05:21 PM||||you get what you pay for,  I have a freind with DOHC cam 3400 .. (four cams) and 1 with a 4.3l  and my 3.2 hangs with both of um (havent took it to the track yet) But the  DOCH3400 definately has an RPM advantage MY.02||||reg||1
Z||000019||Black88GT||08-30-2000||11:19 PM||||I did some minor researching on the DOHC swap and came to the conclusion that it would be a better choice to do the 3.8 N/A or even the 3.8 SC.  The n/a versions can be had for the same amount as a 3.4 DOHC, and the install is about the same level of difficulty.  Also, I heard many people questioning the reliability of the 3.4 DOHC, and they also suggested to me that parts are hard to find and expensive.  The 3.8 has a great reputation and fairly easy/cheap to repairs (but not likely you will need to).  <BR>I am NOT knocking the entrepeneurs who have done the 3.4 DOHC, I am just giving some info.  I did the 3.4 PR because I needed a new engine, I was on a very tight budget and very short time frame.<P><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>'88 GT t-top 3.4<BR>Baltimore, MD||||reg||2
Z||000020||Wannabe||08-31-2000||11:47 AM||||There's little doubt that the 3800 series engines are more reliable, cheaper in the long run and better all around for street use. The 3.4 DOHC is what I'd call a specialized choice. It's an option that provides advantages not available from the other engines, in particular the high top end. I run in Solo 1, which is basically time trials on road race tracks. The ability to hold a gear up to 7000 rpm provides flexibility that can easily shave a second off your lap time if you can save a downshift or or two. From what I've heard, the 3800 engines really shine below 5000 rpm, but drop off above that, so you lose out on the extra 5-7000 useable rpm range. Of course, the other V6-60 engies can be modified to produce good power over 6000 rpm, but the cost will probably be as much or more than putting in a 3.4 DOHC. Again, totally irrelevant on the street, but very useful on the track. ||||reg||1
Z||000021||3400Fiero||08-31-2000||12:24 PM||||The n/a versions can be had for the same amount as a 3.4 DOHC, and the install is about the same level of difficulty<P>I guess that is true if you are going to keep the auto tranny.  From what I understand there were never any 3800 manual combos (correct me if i'm wrong).  I also believe that the 3800 has an 8 bolt crank, which requires a custom flywheel if you are going to run a manual tranny.  That is another reason why I went with the 3400 - in addition to the fact that it is probably lighter - smaller block, aluminum heads.  Obviously there are advantages/disadvantages to each conversion and you have to decide what your priorities are in relation to your budget!<P>------------------<BR>1988 Fiero 3400DOHC<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000022||terryk||09-01-2000||03:43 PM||||The DOHC is good for road racing, not for street racing. The 3800NA or SC is the opposite.<P>I've driven one DOHC Fiero with a Getrag and it felt like a hot 2.8L until 3000 RPM and then it just started to pull. Definitely gets a smile going. Off the line, it was ok.<P>The SC on the otherhand doesn't make you smile, it makes you pucker. And yes, going above 5000-5500 RPM is of no use. It will wind up there (I've run mine up to 6500), but it's pretty weak. Just let the blower and trans do their job. 100 MPH will come up plenty fast.<P>I'm in the middle of both swaps (DOHC/Getrag and 3800SC/4T60E) and I'd say the effort is a wash. The DOHC bolts to the cradle easier whereas the SC doesn't. A dogbone for the DOHC is more difficult, for the SC it's easier. The exhaust issue is about the same. The DOHC needs a bit of trimming on the body for clearance, the SC doesn't. The wiring is a wash. The DOHC will probably cool easier, the SC won't.<BR>||||reg||1
